\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The question}

Let $K/\Q$ be a finite abelian extension of conductor $f$. Class field theory attaches to each
unramified prime $p$ its Artin class, an element of a quotient of $(\Z/f)^\times$, and the splitting
behaviour of $p$ in $K$ is determined by that class. This is a statement of \emph{determination}:
nothing about the splitting type is invisible to the residue $p \bmod f$.

Determination has an information-theoretic shadow. Model the class as a uniform random variable on
the Galois group and let $T$ denote the induced splitting-type read-out. Then determination says
\[
  I(\text{class};T) = H(T),
\]
i.e.\ the class channel is \emph{lossless} for $T$. We call this \textbf{full pinning}. Three
questions present themselves.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Universality.} Empirical surveys at degrees $2,3,5,7,11$ report no exceptions. Is this
a theorem, and under what hypotheses?
\item \textbf{Sharpness.} Is the identity $I = H(T)$ evidence for anything, or would any reasonable
channel produce it?
\item \textbf{Shape.} The rung values $1, 0.9183, 0.7219, 0.5917, 0.4395$ at degrees $2,3,5,7,11$
decrease. Is decay a theorem, and at what rate?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The ladder}

We work with the maximal real cyclotomic subfields
\[
  \Q(\zeta_f)^{+} = \Q\big(\zeta_f + \zeta_f^{-1}\big), \qquad
  \big[\Q(\zeta_f)^{+} : \Q\big] = \tfrac{\varphi(f)}{2},
\]
whose Galois group is $(\Z/f)^\times/\{\pm1\}$. When $f = 2q+1$ and $q$ are both prime the degree is
the prime $q$ and the group is cyclic of order $q$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccl}
\toprule
degree $q$ & conductor $f = 2q+1$ & field \\
\midrule
$2$ & $5$ & $\Q(\zeta_5)^{+} = \Q(\sqrt5)$ \\
$3$ & $7$ & $\Q(\zeta_7)^{+}$ \\
$5$ & $11$ & $\Q(\zeta_{11})^{+}$ \\
$11$ & $23$ & $\Q(\zeta_{23})^{+}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Degree $7$ is realised not by a real cyclotomic field of prime conductor ($15$ is not prime) but by
the unique degree-$7$ subfield of $\Q(\zeta_{29})$, which is abelian and hence covered by the
universal theorem of Section~\ref{sec:universal}. The abstract type channel of
Section~\ref{sec:abstract}, to which the entropy numbers refer, is defined at every prime degree.

\section{The information-theoretic framework}

\subsection{Counting entropy}

\begin{definition}\label{def:uent}
Let $S$ be a nonempty finite set and $g : S \to B$ a read-out. With $N = |S|$ and
$n_{g(x)} = |g^{-1}(g(x))|$ the fibre size through $x$, define
\[
  H(S,g) \;=\; \log_2 N \;-\; \frac1N \sum_{x\in S} \log_2 n_{g(x)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$H(S,g)$ is the Shannon entropy of the push-forward of the uniform measure:
$H(S,g) = -\sum_{v\in g(S)} \frac{n_v}{N}\log_2 \frac{n_v}{N}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Group the sum over $x$ by fibre; each value $v$ contributes $n_v \log_2 n_v$, and
$\log_2 N - \frac1N \sum_v n_v \log_2 n_v = -\sum_v \frac{n_v}{N}\big(\log_2 n_v - \log_2 N\big)$.
\end{proof}

In particular $H(S,g) \ge 0$, with equality exactly when $g$ is constant on $S$.

\begin{definition}
For read-outs $g : S \to B$ and $k : S \to C$,
\[
  H(g\mid k) = \sum_{c\in k(S)} \frac{|k^{-1}(c)|}{|S|}\,H\big(k^{-1}(c),g\big),
  \qquad I(k;g) = H(S,g) - H(g\mid k).
\]
\end{definition}

Conditional entropy is a convex combination of non-negative terms, hence non-negative.

\subsection{Binary entropy in closed form}

\begin{definition}
For $0 < m < N$ put
$B(N,m) = \log_2 N - \dfrac{m\log_2 m + (N-m)\log_2(N-m)}{N}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:binary}
If $g : S \to B$ takes only two values and $m$ is the size of one fibre with $0 < m < N = |S|$, then
$H(S,g) = B(N,m)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Scale invariance]\label{lem:scale}
For every integer $c \ge 1$, $B(cN, cm) = B(N,m)$: the counting entropy depends only on the ratio
$m/N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand with $\log_2(cx) = \log_2 c + \log_2 x$; the $\log_2 c$ terms cancel since
$cm + c(N-m) = cN$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:scale} is the bridge between the abstract cyclic model of order $q$ and the
arithmetic model inside $(\Z/f)^\times$ of order $2q$: the two computations produce literally the
same real number. We use $B(N,1) = \log_2 N - (N-1)\log_2(N-1)/N$.

\subsection{The abstract type channel}\label{sec:abstract}

\begin{definition}
For a modulus $q$ and $a \in \{0,\dots,q-1\}$ let $\type_q(a)$ be the order of $a$ in $\Z/q$, i.e.\
$q/\gcd(a,q)$. The \emph{type entropy} at degree $q$ is
$H(T_q) = H\big(\{0,\dots,q-1\},\type_q\big)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Prime dichotomy]\label{lem:dichotomy}
If $q$ is prime and $a<q$ then $\type_q(a) = 1$ for $a = 0$ and $\type_q(a) = q$ otherwise.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:closedform}
For $q$ prime,
\[
  H(T_q) \;=\; B(q,1) \;=\; \log_2 q - \frac{q-1}{q}\log_2(q-1),
\]
the Shannon entropy of the two-point distribution $\big(1/q,\ (q-1)/q\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:dichotomy} the read-out is two-valued with one distinguished fibre; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:binary} with $N = q$, $m = 1$.
\end{proof}

At $q = 11$ this reads $H(T_{11}) = \log_2 11 - \frac{10}{11}\log_2 10$.

\section{Universal abelian pinning}\label{sec:universal}

\begin{theorem}[Universal abelian pinning]\label{thm:universal}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group, $H \le G$ a subgroup, and define on $G$ the class read-out
$\kappa(u) = uH \in G/H$ and the type read-out $\tau(u) = \ord_{G/H}(uH)$. Then
\[
  H(\tau \mid \kappa) = 0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad I(\kappa;\tau) = H(G,\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\kappa(u) = \kappa(v)$ then $uH = vH$ and hence $\tau(u) = \tau(v)$: the type factors through
the class. Each fibre of $\kappa$ is therefore $\tau$-constant and carries zero entropy, so the
conditional entropy, a convex combination of zeros, vanishes; the mutual information is
$H(G,\tau) - 0$.
\end{proof}

Three comments. First, commutativity is used exactly once, in the setup: in an abelian Galois group
the Frobenius attached to an unramified prime is a single group element rather than a conjugacy
class, the decomposition data of a subfield is a fixed subgroup $H$, and the residue degree is by
definition the order of the image in $G/H$. Second, the theorem ends the empirical programme:
``degrees $2$--$11$, no exceptions'' becomes a corollary, as does every higher abelian degree.
Third, it relocates the frontier: any genuine information defect must be sought where the hypothesis
fails, namely in non-abelian Galois groups.

\subsection{The real-cyclotomic rungs, all at once}

\begin{definition}
For a modulus $f$ let $\Sigma_f = \{\pm1\} \le (\Z/f)^\times$ and for a unit $u$ let the
\emph{real residue degree} $d_f(u)$ be the order of $u\Sigma_f$ in $(\Z/f)^\times/\Sigma_f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:signblind}
$d_f(u) = 1$ if and only if $u = \pm1$; moreover $d_f(-u) = d_f(u)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sigma}
If $f > 2$ is prime then $-1 \ne 1$ in $(\Z/f)^\times$, so $|\Sigma_f| = 2$ and the quotient has
order $(f-1)/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-degree rung]\label{thm:rung}
Let $q$ and $f = 2q+1$ both be prime. Then the Galois group of $\Q(\zeta_f)^{+}$ has order $q$;
every unit satisfies $d_f(u) \in \{1,q\}$; exactly the two units $\pm1$ have $d_f(u) = 1$, giving
split density $1/q$; and
\[
  H\big((\Z/f)^\times, d_f\big) \;=\; H(T_q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The order statement is Lemma~\ref{lem:sigma} with $f - 1 = 2q$. The dichotomy holds because
$d_f(u)$ divides the prime $q$. The split classes are $\pm1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:signblind}. Finally
$d_f$ is two-valued with distinguished fibre of size $2$ in a set of size $2q$, so its entropy is
$B(2q,2) = B(q,1) = H(T_q)$ by Lemmas~\ref{lem:binary},~\ref{lem:scale} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:closedform}.
\end{proof}

Instantiating at $(q,f) \in \{(2,5),(3,7),(5,11),(11,23)\}$ yields the rungs of degree $2,3,5,11$,
the degree-$11$ rung $\Q(\zeta_{23})^{+}$ included, as corollaries.

\section{The degree-11 rung in detail}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting criterion at degree 11]\label{thm:crit11}
Let $p$ be coprime to $23$. Then $p$ splits completely in $\Q(\zeta_{23})^{+}$, i.e.\
$d_{23}(p) = 1$, if and only if $p \equiv 1$ or $p \equiv -1 \pmod{23}$. Otherwise
$d_{23}(p) = 11$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\big|(\Z/23)^\times\big| = 22$ and $|\Sigma_{23}| = 2$, so the quotient has prime order $11$; the
order of any class is $1$ or $11$, and it is $1$ exactly when the class lies in $\{\pm1\}$.
\end{proof}

For instance $47 \equiv 1 \bmod 23$ splits completely, while $2 \not\equiv \pm1$ is inert with
$d_{23}(2) = 11$. In coordinates, fixing a primitive root $g$ and writing $p \equiv g^a$, we have
$\{\pm1\} = \{g^0, g^{11}\}$, so $p$ splits iff $a \equiv 0 \pmod{11}$.

\begin{theorem}[Certified bracket]\label{thm:bracket}
$0.4394 < H(T_{11}) < 0.4396$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $R = 11^{11}/10^{10}$, so $\log_2 R = 11\,H(T_{11})$. The integer inequalities
\[
  2^{2417}\cdot 10^{5000} < 11^{5500}, \qquad 11^{2200} < 2^{967}\cdot 10^{2000}
\]
give $2^{2417} < R^{500}$ and $R^{200} < 2^{967}$, i.e.\ $2417 < 500\log_2 R$ and
$200\log_2 R < 967$. Dividing by $5500$ and $2200$ respectively yields the bracket. No
floating-point arithmetic is involved.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Full pinning at degree 11]
Let $\sigma(u) = \{u,-u\}$ be the sign class of a unit mod $23$, a strictly coarser observable than
$u$ itself. Then $H(d_{23}\mid \sigma) = 0$ and $I(\sigma; d_{23}) = H(T_{11}) = 0.4395\ldots$
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\sigma(u) = \sigma(v)$ forces $v = \pm u$, and $d_{23}$ is sign-blind by
Lemma~\ref{lem:signblind}, so the type factors through $\sigma$; apply the mechanism of
Theorem~\ref{thm:universal} and evaluate with Theorem~\ref{thm:rung}.
\end{proof}

\section{Sharpness: pinning is exactly measurability}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:pos}
Let $S$ be finite and $g : S \to B$. If $g(x) \ne g(y)$ for some $x,y \in S$, then $H(S,g) > 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $N = |S| \ge 2$. For any $a \in S$ the fibre through $a$ omits at least one of $x,y$: if
$g(a) = g(x)$ it cannot contain $y$, and otherwise it cannot contain $x$. Hence every fibre has size
at most $N-1$, and fibres are nonempty, so
$\frac1N \sum_{a} \log_2 |g^{-1}(g(a))| \le \log_2(N-1) < \log_2 N$. Subtracting from $\log_2 N$
gives $H(S,g) > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pinning criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
For finite $S$ and read-outs $g,k$,
\[
  H(g\mid k) = 0 \iff \forall x,y \in S:\ k(x) = k(y) \Rightarrow g(x) = g(y).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Each fibre of $k$ is $g$-constant, hence of zero entropy. ($\Rightarrow$) If
$k(x) = k(y)$ but $g(x) \ne g(y)$, the fibre $F = k^{-1}(k(x))$ contains both points, so
$H(F,g) > 0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:pos}; its weight $|F|/|S|$ is positive and all other terms are
non-negative, so $H(g\mid k) > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Dichotomy]
$I(k;g) = H(S,g)$ if and only if $g$ factors through $k$; otherwise $I(k;g) < H(S,g)$ strictly. An
observed identity $I = H$ is thus never a numerical near-coincidence.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Criterion at degree 11]\label{cor:crit11}
For any channel $k$ on $(\Z/23)^\times$, $I(k;d_{23}) = H(T_{11})$ holds precisely when $k$ never
merges a split class with an inert class; if it merges even one such pair, then
$I(k;d_{23}) < H(T_{11})$ strictly.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Legendre pinning failure]
Let $\chi(u)$ indicate whether $u$ is a square in $(\Z/23)^\times$. Then
$I(\chi;d_{23}) < H(T_{11})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$5^2 = 25 \equiv 2 \pmod{23}$, so $2$ is a quadratic residue, as is $1$ trivially; thus
$\chi(2) = \chi(1)$. But $d_{23}(1) = 1$ while $d_{23}(2) = 11$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:crit11}. So
$\chi$ merges a split with an inert class, and Corollary~\ref{cor:crit11} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero information from the quadratic character]\label{thm:zeroinfo}
In exponent coordinates, with $a \in \Z/22$, let $\tau(a) = \type_{11}(a \bmod 11)$ and
$\chi(a) = a \bmod 2$. Then $I(\chi;\tau) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exactly the exponents $a \in \{0,11\}$ have $\tau(a) = 1$, so the unconditional entropy is
$B(22,2)$. Each fibre of $\chi$ consists of $11$ exponents and contains exactly one multiple of
$11$ ($0$ in the even fibre, $11$ in the odd one), so each conditional entropy is $B(11,1)$, and so
is their equal-weight average. Finally $B(22,2) = B(11,1)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:scale}.
\end{proof}

Conceptually this is a Chinese-Remainder splitting $\Z/22 \cong \Z/2 \times \Z/11$: the type sees
only the $\Z/11$ coordinate, the quadratic character only the $\Z/2$ coordinate, and
$\gcd(2,11) = 1$ makes them independent. The same argument applies at every odd prime degree. Thus
at degree $11$ two natural channels sit at opposite extremes: the sign class transmits all
$0.4395$ bits, the Legendre symbol none.

\section{Semiprimes: an exact binomial law and its channel}

Let $q$ be a prime degree and model a semiprime $N = p_1p_2$ by the pair $(a_1,a_2)$ of exponents of
its factors, ranging over the box $\{0,\dots,q-1\}^2$ of size $q^2$. The split count is
\[
  s(a_1,a_2) = \#\{i : \type_q(a_i) = 1\} = [a_1 = 0] + [a_2 = 0] \in \{0,1,2\}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Exact $\Bin(2,1/q)$ law]\label{thm:binomial}
For prime $q$, among the $q^2$ pairs,
\[
  \#\{s=2\} = 1, \qquad \#\{s=1\} = 2(q-1), \qquad \#\{s=0\} = (q-1)^2,
\]
and $1 + 2(q-1) + (q-1)^2 = q^2$. At $q = 11$ the counts are $(1,20,100)$ out of $121$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional split-count entropy]
For prime $q$,
\[
  H(\text{box}, s) = \log_2(q^2)
    - \frac{(q-1)^2\log_2\!\big((q-1)^2\big) + 2(q-1)\log_2\!\big(2(q-1)\big)}{q^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Fibre structure]\label{lem:fibres}
Let $\rho(a_1,a_2) = (a_1+a_2) \bmod q$ be the class of the product. Each fibre $\rho^{-1}(c)$ has
$q$ elements. On the fibre $c = 0$ the split count is $2$ at $(0,0)$ and $0$ elsewhere; on a fibre
$c \ne 0$ exactly the pairs $(0,c)$ and $(c,0)$ have split count $1$ and the other $q-2$ have $0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional split-count entropy]
For prime $q$, $\;H(s \mid \rho) = \frac1q B(q,1) + \frac{q-1}{q}B(q,2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fibres} the zero fibre is a $1$-against-$(q-1)$ split and each of the $q-1$
nonzero fibres is a $2$-against-$(q-2)$ split; each of the $q$ fibres has weight $q/q^2 = 1/q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form of the split-count channel]\label{thm:isplit}
For prime $q$,
\[
  \Isplit(q) = \log_2(q^2)
   - \frac{(q-1)^2\log_2\!\big((q-1)^2\big) + 2(q-1)\log_2\!\big(2(q-1)\big)}{q^2}
   - \left[\frac1q B(q,1) + \frac{q-1}{q}B(q,2)\right].
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The degree-11 value]\label{thm:isplit11}
\[
  \Isplit(11) = \log_2 11 + \frac{180\log_2 3 - 210\log_2 5 - 210}{121},
\]
and $0.0516 < \Isplit(11) < 0.0521$; numerically $\Isplit(11) = 0.05190\ldots$ bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substituting $q = 11$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:isplit}, all logarithms reduce via
$\log_2 121 = 2\log_2 11$, $\log_2 100 = 2 + 2\log_2 5$, $\log_2 20 = 2 + \log_2 5$,
$\log_2 10 = 1 + \log_2 5$, $\log_2 9 = 2\log_2 3$. Setting
$A = 121\log_2 11 + 180\log_2 3 - 210\log_2 5$ gives $\Isplit(11) = (A-210)/121$, and the bounds
$865 < 4A$ and $10A < 2163$ follow from the integer inequalities
$2^{865}\cdot 5^{840} < 11^{484}\cdot 3^{720}$ and
$11^{1210}\cdot 3^{1800} < 2^{2163}\cdot 5^{2100}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[A corrected prediction]
A value of $0.116$ bits had been announced in advance for this quantity. Under the definitions used
here --- mutual information between the number of split prime factors of a semiprime and the class
of the semiprime itself --- the true value is $0.0519\ldots$, less than half the announced figure,
and $\Isplit(11) < 0.0521 < 0.116$ is certified by integer arithmetic.
\end{remark}

\section{The shape of the ladder}

\begin{theorem}[Decay sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For every prime $q$,
\[
  \frac{\log_2 q}{q} \;\le\; H(T_q) \;\le\; \frac{\log_2 q + 1/\ln 2}{q}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rewrite $H(T_q) = \frac{\log_2 q}{q} + \frac{q-1}{q}\big(\log_2 q - \log_2(q-1)\big)$. The
correction term is non-negative, giving the lower bound; and by
$\log_2(x+1) - \log_2 x \le 1/(x\ln 2)$ with $x = q-1$ it is at most $1/(q\ln2)$.
\end{proof}

The exact excess is $q H(T_q) - \log_2 q = (q-1)\log_2\!\big(q/(q-1)\big) \nearrow 1/\ln 2$, so the
upper bound is asymptotically sharp and the lower bound is never attained.

\begin{corollary}
For prime $q \ne 2$ we have $H(T_q) < 1$: only degree $2$ reaches a full bit. For $q \ge 4$ this
follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} together with $\log_2 q \le q/2$ (valid since
$q^2 \le 2^q$ for $q \ge 4$) and $1/\ln 2 < 1.4427$; for $q = 3$ it is $\log_2 3 < 5/3$, i.e.\
$3^3 < 2^5$. Moreover $H(T_q) \ge \log_2 q/q > 0$, so no rung is silent.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}
For real $x>1$ let $h(x) = \log_2 x - \dfrac{x-1}{x}\log_2(x-1)$.
\end{definition}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:closedform}, $h(q) = H(T_q)$ at every prime $q$, and $h(2) = 1$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact derivative]\label{thm:deriv}
For $x > 1$,
\[
  h'(x) \;=\; -\,\frac{\ln(x-1)}{x^{2}\ln 2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $h(x) = \big(\ln x - \frac{x-1}{x}\ln(x-1)\big)/\ln 2$. Since
$\frac{d}{dx}\frac{x-1}{x} = 1/x^2$ and $\frac{d}{dx}\ln(x-1) = 1/(x-1)$, the product rule gives
\[
  \frac{d}{dx}\left[\frac{x-1}{x}\ln(x-1)\right] = \frac{\ln(x-1)}{x^2} + \frac1x,
\]
so $\ln 2 \cdot h'(x) = \frac1x - \frac{\ln(x-1)}{x^2} - \frac1x = -\frac{\ln(x-1)}{x^2}$.
\end{proof}

The cancellation of the two $1/x$ terms is the whole content: the derivative is a single logarithm.

\begin{theorem}[Strict antitonicity]\label{thm:anti}
$h$ is strictly decreasing on $[2,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$h$ is differentiable, hence continuous, on $(1,\infty)$. For $x > 2$ we have $\ln(x-1) > 0$ and
$x^2\ln 2 > 0$, so $h'(x) < 0$ on the interior of $[2,\infty)$; a continuous function with negative
derivative on the interior of an interval is strictly decreasing there.
\end{proof}

Note $h'(2) = 0$ exactly: the profile is flat at the bottom rung and falls away thereafter, which is
the analytic reason degree $2$ is distinguished.

\begin{theorem}[Strict decay of the ladder]\label{thm:decay}
For primes $p < q$ we have $H(T_q) < H(T_p)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both $p,q \in [2,\infty)$ with $p < q$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:anti} and $h(p) = H(T_p)$,
$h(q) = H(T_q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$H(T_{11}) < H(T_7) < H(T_5) < H(T_3) < H(T_2) = 1$, numerically
$0.4395 < 0.5917 < 0.7219 < 0.9183 < 1$. The abelian ladder is a strictly decreasing sequence of
strictly positive entropies tending to $0$ at the rate $\log_2 q/q$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Discussion}

The two halves of this paper pull in opposite directions, and their combination is the result.
Theorem~\ref{thm:universal} says the arithmetic-to-information channel is never lossy in the abelian
world: the residue class transmits every bit of the splitting type, at every degree, in every
abelian field. Theorem~\ref{thm:decay} says the message gets quieter without exception, with
$H(T_q) \sim \log_2 q/q \to 0$. The abelian ladder thus exhibits perfect transmission of a vanishing
signal: complete splitting becomes rare (density $1/q$), and rarity is informationally cheap. The
channel's perfection is structural; the signal's decay is measure-theoretic.

The sharpness results keep this from being vacuous. Because $H(T\mid k) = 0$ is \emph{equivalent} to
measurability (Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}), pinning is a genuine binary property of a channel, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:zeroinfo} exhibits a perfectly natural arithmetic channel at degree $11$ on the
wrong side of it, with mutual information exactly zero.

The semiprime results have a plain cryptographic reading. Given only $N = p_1p_2$ and its residue
mod $23$, an observer learns about the splitting behaviour of the hidden factors only
$\Isplit(11) = 0.052$ bits, whereas the factors' individual types would be worth
$2 \times 0.4395 = 0.879$ bits; conditioning on the product destroys almost all of it.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{Non-abelian pinning defect.} Theorem~\ref{thm:universal} uses commutativity exactly once: to
know that the Frobenius class is a single group element. For a non-abelian Galois group the Artin
symbol is a conjugacy class, and while the residue degree remains class-determined, the honest place
to look for a defect is the abelianised channel, where a genuine gap can open. The first real test
is the smallest non-abelian Galois group $S_3$, the splitting field of $x^3-2$, small enough to
enumerate exactly.

\textbf{The second-order ladder law.} Rung-by-rung monotonicity is now a theorem, so the question
moves one order down: $q H(T_q) - \log_2 q = (q-1)\log_2\!\big(q/(q-1)\big)$ is elementary and
increases to $1/\ln 2 = 1.4427\ldots$, so the sandwich is asymptotically sharp at the upper end and
the lower bound is never attained. With the exact derivative in hand the remaining step is a
monotone-limit statement about $(1+1/t)^t$.

\textbf{Composite degrees and the $\varphi$-profile.} At composite degree $n$ the type is no longer
two-valued: the order of a class ranges over the divisors of $n$, the divisor $d$ occurring
$\varphi(d)$ times, so the binary entropy is replaced by
$H(T_n) = -\sum_{d\mid n}\frac{\varphi(d)}{n}\log_2\frac{\varphi(d)}{n}$, of which the split density
$1/n$ is only the first term.

\textbf{Beyond cyclic quotients.} The universal theorem covers arbitrary finite abelian $G$ and
arbitrary $H$, so non-cyclic Galois groups are already pinned; what is not yet computed is the
entropy profile of their type read-outs, where several residue degrees coexist with multiplicities
governed by the subgroup lattice.

\section{Conclusion}

The degree-$11$ rung, realised by $\Q(\zeta_{23})^{+}$, confirms every structural prediction made of
it: primes split completely exactly when $p \equiv \pm1 \bmod 23$; the split density is $1/11$,
giving $H(T) = \log_2 11 - \frac{10}{11}\log_2 10 \in (0.4394, 0.4396)$; the residue channel is
fully pinned; and the semiprime split count follows $\Bin(2,1/11)$ with exact counts $(100,20,1)$
out of $121$. The one numerical prediction that fails, the split-count channel value, is replaced by
a closed form and a certified bracket. More importantly, the rung is the last one that needed
checking: pinning for abelian Galois groups is a theorem with no exceptions, its sharpness criterion
is exactly pointwise measurability, and the ladder's decay follows from the single-logarithm
derivative $h'(x) = -\ln(x-1)/(x^2\ln2)$, which vanishes exactly at $x = 2$ and is negative
thereafter. The abelian ladder is closed; the frontier is non-abelian.

